    </section>

    <section>
      <title>Functions</title>

      <p>Today will be about functions, but to do this, I need to review some of the essential properties and notation surrounding sets and functions.</p>

      <p>First if I have a function from a set <m>R</m> to a set <m>S</m>, I write it as <m>f: R \to S</m>. Then <m>R</m> is called the <term>domain</term> of the function, and <m>S</m> is called the <term>co-domain</term> of the function. This is in contrast with the <term>range</term>, which is the set of values the function can take.</p>

      <p>So for example, given two vectors <m>(1,0,1,0)</m> and <m>(0,1,0,1)</m>, we can define a function <m>T: \mathbb{R}^{2} \to \mathbb{R}^{4}</m> by</p>
      <md>T(x,y) = x(1,0,1,0) + y(0,1,0,1) = \begin{pmatrix} 1 &amp; 0 \\ 0 &amp; 1 \\ 1 &amp; 0 \\ 0 &amp; 1 \end{pmatrix} \begin{pmatrix} x \\ y\end{pmatrix}.</md>
      <p>The domain of <m>T</m> is <m>\mathbb{R}^{2}</m>, the co-domain of <m>T</m> is <m>\mathbb{R}^{4}</m>, and the range of <m>T</m> is the span of <m>(1,0,1,0)</m> and <m>(0,1,0,1)</m>, which is a <m>2</m>-D plane in <m>\mathbb{R}^{4}</m>.</p>

      <p>Given a point in the co-domain, we can ask for the <term>pre-image</term> of that point. For a function <m>f: R \to S</m>, the pre-image of a point <m>b</m> in <m>S</m> is non-empty if and only if <m>b</m> is in the range.</p>

      <p>For example, asking for the pre-image of <m>(1,2,3,4)</m> under <m>T</m> is asking for solutions to the linear system</p>
      <md>x(1,0,1,0) + y(0,1,0,1) = T(x,y) = (1,2,3,4).</md>
      <p>A solution to this linear system exists if and only if <m>(1,2,3,4)</m> is in the range of <m>T</m>. The set of all solutions to the system is the pre-image of <m>(1,2,3,4)</m> with respect to <m>T</m>.</p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <title>Transforming</title>

      <p>The core question of the day is: Which functions <m>T: \mathbb{R}^{n} \to \mathbb{R}^{m}</m> may be written as <m>T(\vec{x}) = A \vec{x}</m>?</p>

      <p>What properties does this function have?</p>

      <p>It is <term>additive</term>:</p>
      <md>T(\vec{x} + \vec{y}) = A(\vec{x}+\vec{y}) = A\vec{x} + A\vec{y} = T(\vec{x}) + T(\vec{y}).</md>

      <p>It respects <term>scaling</term>:</p>
      <md>T(\lambda \vec{x}) = A(\lambda \vec{x}) = \lambda A\vec{x} = \lambda T(\vec{x}).</md>

      <definition>
        <statement>
          <p>We call a function <m>f: \mathbb{R}^{n} \to \mathbb{R}^{m}</m> a <term>linear transformation</term> if <m>f</m> is additive and respects scaling.</p>
        </statement>
      </definition>

      <theorem>
        <statement>
          <p>A function <m>T: \mathbb{R}^{n} \to \mathbb{R}^{m}</m> is a linear transformation if and only if <m>T(x) = Ax</m> for some <m>m \times n</m> matrix <m>A</m>.</p>
        </statement>
      </theorem>

      <p>Let's start in one dimension. Then <m>T: \mathbb{R} \to \mathbb{R}</m>. A matrix in this setting corresponds to just multiplying by a number, so we want to show that <m>T(x) = mx</m> for some <m>m \in \mathbb{R}</m>.</p>

      <p>Define <m>m = T(1)</m>. Then for all <m>x \in \mathbb{R}</m>,</p>
      <md>T(x) = xT(1) = mx.</md>

      <p>For higher dimensions, a similar argument works. Let <m>T: \mathbb{R}^{2} \to \mathbb{R}</m>. We would want to write this as</p>
      <md>T(x,y) = \begin{pmatrix} m_{1} &amp; m_{2} \end{pmatrix}\begin{pmatrix} x \\ y \end{pmatrix} = m_{1} x + m_{2}y.</md>
      <p>Define <m>m_{1} = T(1,0)</m> and <m>m_{2} = T(0,1)</m>. Then</p>
      <md>T(x,y) = T(x(1,0) + y(0,1)) = xT(1,0) + y T(0,1) = m_{1} x+ m_{2}y.</md>

      <p>It turns out this same argument works in general. We will just use some notation to make it work. Let <m>\vec{e_{i}}</m> be the vector <m>1</m> in the <m>i</m>th coordinate and zeroes otherwise.</p>

      <p>Define <m>T: \mathbb{R}^{n} \to \mathbb{R}^{m}</m>, and suppose that <m>T</m> is a linear transformation. Then</p>
      <md>\begin{aligned}
        T(x_{1},x_{2},\dots,x_{n}) &amp;= T(\sum_{i=1}^{n} x_{i} e_{i}) \\
        &amp;= \sum_{i=1}^{n} x_{i}T(e_{i}) \\
        &amp;= \begin{pmatrix} T(e_{1}) &amp; T(e_{2}) &amp; \dots &amp; T(e_{n}) \end{pmatrix} \begin{pmatrix} x_{1} \\ x_{2} \\ \vdots \\ x_{n} \end{pmatrix}.
      \end{aligned}</md>

